<section class="ab" aria-labelledby="experiment">
<div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Experiment</p>
    <h2 id="experiment">What does a curtain change?</h2>
    <p class="text">The same room, the same cold outside, only a curtain is added. The warm air from the radiator then turns into the room sooner, and the cold glass sits behind the fabric.</p>
    <div class="pair">
        <figure><figcaption><strong>Without a curtain</strong> Cool air sinks along the glass. The warm air from the radiator rises at an angle into the room.</figcaption>{{ bild("exp-ohne-vorhang", "en", "Room view without a curtain: a radiator below the window, cool air sinking along the glass, and the warm air from the radiator rising at an angle into the room, in the model.", 465, 930, 0.95092) }}</figure>
        <figure><figcaption><strong>With a curtain</strong> The warm air from the radiator flows flat into the room below the hem. The glass sits behind the fabric.</figcaption>{{ bild("exp-mit-vorhang", "en", "The same room view with a curtain down to the windowsill: the glass behind the fabric, and below the hem the warm air from the radiator flowing flat into the room, in the model.", 465, 930, 0.95092) }}</figure>
    </div>
    <p class="pair-meta"><span class="badge">2D simulation · qualitative</span> <span class="legend"><span>cooler</span> <span class="scale" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>warmer</span></span> Both pictures use the same fixed color scale.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="ab" aria-labelledby="wall">
<div class="wrap split">
    <div class="kopf">
        <p class="kicker">Wall view</p>
        <h2 id="wall">How cool does the wall get?</h2>
    </div>
    <figure class="bild-f">{{ bild("wand", "en", "Wall view: a section through the outside wall with the temperature line from inside to outside, the mold risk and dew point lines and the level beside it, in the model.", 760, 1440, 1.33333) }}<figcaption>The outside wall in section: mold risk in three levels.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="rest">
        <p class="text">The second view cuts through the outside wall. You see how the temperature drops from inside to outside and how damp the surface gets. From that, it shows the mold risk in three levels:</p>
        <ol class="levels">
            <li><strong>low</strong> The wall stays warm on the inside; in the model, the surface hardly gets damp.</li>
            <li><strong>elevated</strong> The wall gets cooler, and the surface gets damp enough for some kinds of mold.</li>
            <li><strong>high</strong> The wall is so cool on the inside that the surface gets damp enough for many kinds of mold, if it stays this cold for a long time.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text">Choose a wall insulated on the outside, and the inside gets warmer. Lower the room humidity, and the mold risk line moves down. The level follows at once.</p>
        <p><span class="badge">Wall · heat conduction · 3 risk levels</span></p>
    </div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="ab" aria-labelledby="physik">
<div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker">What is calculated</p>
    <h2 id="physik">Calculated live on your iPhone.</h2>
    <p class="text">Behind every picture is a calculation, not an animation. The app works out three things for you:</p>
    <figure class="gross">{{ bild("substanz-sofa", "en", "Room view: a sofa below the window, open underneath, and the air between sofa and radiator, in the model.", 760, 1206, 1.00396) }}<figcaption>A sofa below the window, open underneath: how does the air move in the model?</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="tiles">
        <section class="tile"><span class="sign sign-air" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3>Air flow</h3><p>How warm air rises above the radiator, cool air sinks at the window, and air moves around curtain and sofa.</p></section>
        <section class="tile"><span class="sign sign-temp" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3>Temperature</h3><p>Where the air in the room is warmer or cooler, and what changes when you rearrange something.</p></section>
        <section class="tile"><span class="sign sign-wall" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3>Heat conduction</h3><p>How heat flows through the wall layer by layer and how warm the inside stays.</p></section>
    </div>
</div>
</section>

